WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.330
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:08.440
What took us from living in caves to being
an advanced, multi-lingual, intelligent species?

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:14.139
It has a lot to do with just a handful of
extremely crucial inventions.

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:19.130
Let's take a look at the incredible inventions
that played the biggest parts in advancing

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.380
the human race.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.050
1.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:27.650
Fist up is fire.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:31.790
Okay it's not strictly an invention, more
a discovery.

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:38.320
But being the first source of warmth for humans,
it's importance cannot go un-noted.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.680
It's estimated that fire was discovered 1.8
million years ago.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:48.860
And it set the wheels in motion, as it was
the basis for energy for years to come.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:49.860
2.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.080
Speaking of wheels.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:57.380
It wasn't until 3,500 BC when humans finally
invented the wheel.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:03.580
The wheel proved to be such a crucial component
of the advancement of the human race, so why

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.000
did it take humans so long to invent it?

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:14.210
I mean by 3500 BC it was already the Bronze
Age, humans were casting metal alloys, building

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:19.970
sailboats and even complex musical instruments
such as the harp.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:23.030
So what took them so long.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:28.480
Well the problem with the wheel, was not getting
a cylindrical object to roll on its side,

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.159
anyone could do that.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:36.670
It was attaching that cylinder to a stationary
platform, so it could move freely and independently,

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.130
without rolling off down the hill.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:44.601
This is known as the wheel and axle design,
to achieve this a lot of factors have to be

00:01:44.601 --> 00:01:45.970
gotten right.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:52.340
Such as, the circumference of the axels has
to be perfectly smooth, as does the hole in

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:56.950
the centre of the wheel, otherwise there will
be too much friction and the wheels won't

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.509
turn.

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:05.479
Even more challenging, the axels had to fit
snuggly inside the wheels, but not too snuggly

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:08.170
or they won't rotate freely.

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:14.152
Inventors would also need access to very large
slabs of wood from a large tree in order to

00:02:14.152 --> 00:02:16.210
carve a large wheel from it.

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:21.370
The wheel was certainly a huge challenge;
all these factors and more are why humans

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:24.190
took so long to invent the wheel.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:29.870
It's not something that would have happened
overnight and it likely only happened once,

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:35.459
but once it was discovered, it spread very
quickly over the rest of Europe.

00:02:35.459 --> 00:02:40.349
The wheel's most likely birthplace was either
Eastern Europe or Mesopotamia, but historians

00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:46.319
can't agree on a precise location.

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:52.060
The wheel was so vitally important because
it gave birth to modern day farming, which

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:58.730
made us less nomadic and freed up people's
time so they could focus on discovering new

00:02:58.730 --> 00:02:59.730
inventions.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:00.730
3.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:05.879
Imagine you're far out at sea, traveling to
a far off land, the ocean currents are pulling

00:03:05.879 --> 00:03:12.849
your boat in every direction, you no longer
know which way is north, south, east or west,

00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:15.810
you have nothing but your boat to guide you.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:16.920
What happens?

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.260
You will probably end up going gravely off
course, or even more likely just go round

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:27.090
and round in circles for days, until you run
out of water and die at sea.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:32.020
This was what happened to many a sailor in
the thousands of years between the invention

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:36.190
of the boat and a navigational device.

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:41.420
The world was full of people getting lost
at sea and the whole thing was a bloody mess.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:48.030
Until the 11th century when Han dynasty China
invented the very first maritime compass.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:53.340
The first compass used a lodestone, which
is a naturally magnetised iron ore.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:58.659
The compass quickly spread throughout the
rest of the world via maritime contact.

00:03:58.659 --> 00:04:05.280
And so finally people could safely traverse
the seas, and travel far away from their homelands

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:11.140
to distant new worlds, opening up new trade
routes and starting the Age of Discovery.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:12.140
4.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:13.140
Knowledge is power.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:17.489
It was the desire for knowledge that took
us from being hunter gatherers to where we

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:19.129
are now.

00:04:19.129 --> 00:04:25.330
Nothing excels the technological advancement
of a species more than the sharing of knowledge.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:30.639
But in the 15th century how would you go about
sharing knowledge?

00:04:30.639 --> 00:04:32.389
There's no internet or phones.

00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:34.910
So people relied on books.

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:41.250
But for many centuries books had to be painstakingly
hand written, one by one.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:46.310
This meant book were very expensive and knowledge
was severely limited for the majority of the

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:52.050
population and for a long time this proved
to be a major brick wall, standing in the

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:54.889
way of the progress of mankind.

00:04:54.889 --> 00:05:00.699
What was really needed was an abundance of
books so human knowledge could be shared freely

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:01.889
and quickly.

00:05:01.889 --> 00:05:07.889
And that's what the much needed printing press
gave to the human race when it was invented

00:05:07.889 --> 00:05:09.389
in the 15th century.

00:05:09.389 --> 00:05:16.389
Although the first movable type system was
created in 11th century Song Dynasty China,

00:05:16.389 --> 00:05:22.029
the complexity of the Chinese writing system
stopped it from gaining much traction.

00:05:22.029 --> 00:05:28.649
It wasn't until the 15th century when Johannes
Gutenberg invented the first western movable

00:05:28.649 --> 00:05:36.360
type printing press in Germany, that the idea
of mass printing really caught on.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:42.410
Gutenberg cast individual letters and punctuation
marks out of various metals, which was a vast

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:47.020
improvement on the Chinese system that used
wooden blocks.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:51.740
Gutenberg's printing press exponentially increased
the speed at which copies of books could be

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:57.159
made, which led to a rapid increase in the
spread of knowledge across Europe.

00:05:57.159 --> 00:05:58.159
5.

00:05:58.159 --> 00:06:04.759
In 1859 a Belgian engineer called Étienne
Lenoir created one of the most revolutionary

00:06:04.759 --> 00:06:10.539
pieces of engineering to date, the internal
combustion engine.

00:06:10.539 --> 00:06:17.259
This engineering masterpiece replaced the
dated steam engine to become the de-facto

00:06:17.259 --> 00:06:22.240
standard for powering the world's machinery
and transportation systems.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:27.919
The internal combustion engine works by burning
fuel in a combustion chamber, the high pressure

00:06:27.919 --> 00:06:34.440
gas which is created then pushes a piston,
thereby transforming chemical energy into

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.080
useful mechanical energy.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:42.180
The internal combustion engine gave birth
to countless modern technologies but most

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:45.979
notably modern cars and aircraft.

00:06:45.979 --> 00:06:47.189
6.

00:06:47.189 --> 00:06:48.740
Next up is the telephone.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:55.419
Although it had many pioneers it was Alexander
Graham Bell who was awarded the first patent

00:06:55.419 --> 00:06:59.199
for the electric telephone in 1876.

00:06:59.199 --> 00:07:05.259
Bell's goal was to make a telephone that was
affordable to everyone, before this electric

00:07:05.259 --> 00:07:10.879
communication such as the electric telegraph
were horrendously expensive and were only

00:07:10.879 --> 00:07:13.089
used by the very wealthy.

00:07:13.089 --> 00:07:19.499
Bell changed this, and very soon there was
a telephone in nearly every home, business

00:07:19.499 --> 00:07:21.809
and learning institution.

00:07:21.809 --> 00:07:27.330
This enabled instantaneous communication and
revolutionised the way we communicate with

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:29.139
the rest of the world.

00:07:29.139 --> 00:07:34.729
The telephone catalysed international business
and accelerated human learning.

00:07:34.729 --> 00:07:40.309
In later years the telephone system would
also become the building blocks for the internet.

00:07:40.309 --> 00:07:41.309
7.

00:07:41.309 --> 00:07:48.489
Next up is the very foundation that powers
the entire world we live in today, electricity.

00:07:48.489 --> 00:07:52.389
Human's have always been aware of electricity
and its power.

00:07:52.389 --> 00:08:00.400
Ancient Egyptian texts dating back to 2750
BC talk about people getting shocks from electric

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:04.080
fish such as Catfish and the Electric Ray.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:10.399
But it wasn't until Thomas Edison created
the first practical, long-lasting electric

00:08:10.399 --> 00:08:17.860
light bulb in 1879, that electricity transformed
from being just a scientific curiosity to

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:21.309
an essential tool for modern life.

00:08:21.309 --> 00:08:27.500
Following Edison's invention a whole multitude
of other electrical devices quickly followed

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:29.849
in the years to come.

00:08:29.849 --> 00:08:34.879
Nothing has altered the face of technology
like the discovery and practical applications

00:08:34.879 --> 00:08:36.779
of electrical power.

00:08:36.779 --> 00:08:37.779
8.

00:08:37.779 --> 00:08:44.190
In 1928 a Scottish scientist called Alexander
Fleming was experimenting with bacteria in

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:49.310
his laboratory when he discovered something
that would change the world.

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:55.149
He noticed that one of his petri dishes filled
with bacteria had become contaminated with

00:08:55.149 --> 00:09:01.240
mould, and everywhere the mould had spread
the bacteria was dead.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:06.970
What was this mysterious mould that had the
ability to stop bacteria dead in its tracks,

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:11.029
it turned out to be a fungus called Penicillium.

00:09:11.029 --> 00:09:17.019
Over the next two decades chemists purified
the fungus and eventually turned it into the

00:09:17.019 --> 00:09:21.060
drug penicillin, the worlds first antibiotic.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:27.399
Penicillin proved to be the magic bullet for
a whole load of previously deadly diseases,

00:09:27.399 --> 00:09:34.180
this new miracle cure could fight off infections
without doing any harm to most humans.

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:38.630
Fleming's discovery forever changed the way
we treat diseases.

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:39.720
9.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:46.100
British mathematician and inventor Charles
Babbage invented the first computer in the

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:53.550
year 1832 it contained processors which Babbage
called "mills" and a memory which he called

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:56.750
the "store", just like in modern computers.

00:09:56.750 --> 00:10:03.519
But unfortunately Babbage was way ahead of
his time, it wouldn't be until over 100 years

00:10:03.519 --> 00:10:11.649
later in 1971 when the world's first personal
computer was manufactured and sold.

00:10:11.649 --> 00:10:17.760
It was called the Kenbak-1 and sold for $750.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:24.250
Ever since then computer technology has accelerated
at an unprecedented rate and subsequently

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:29.689
so has human knowledge and our technological
accomplishments.

00:10:29.689 --> 00:10:34.550
Just like the abacus did thousands of years
ago, the personal computer outsourced the

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:39.940
task of complex mathematics and problem solving
to machines.

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:47.290
The computer was so influential to human development
that it accelerated us into a brand new age,

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:49.040
the information age.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:50.040
10.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:56.420
And finally, you can't talk about the personal
computer, without, of course talking about

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:57.860
the internet.

00:10:57.860 --> 00:11:04.430
No other invention has shaped the way we go
about our daily lives, as much as the internet.

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:10.949
We now heavily rely on the internet for shopping,
business, relationships, entertainment, creativity

00:11:10.949 --> 00:11:14.439
and general human communications.

00:11:14.439 --> 00:11:22.180
English computer scientist Sir Tim Berners-Lee
invented the World Wide Web in 1989 whilst

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:30.940
working at CERN, he then wrote the very first
HTTP web client and server in 1990.

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:36.949
Berners-Lee also founded the World Wide Web
Consortium, which is responsible for maintaining

00:11:36.949 --> 00:11:46.010
the international standards for URLs, HTTP,
HTML and CSS, or to put it more simply, the

00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:49.120
technology that the entire internet runs on.

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:53.560
We still use those same standards and technologies
to this day.

00:11:53.560 --> 00:12:00.820
So, in a movement that could be considered
both scary and exciting at the very same time,

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:07.649
the internet changed human kind like nothing
before it, and as the internet and the way

00:12:07.649 --> 00:12:12.750
we use it evolves every year, so too, does
the human race.

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:16.019
Thanks for the view, subscribe for more Thoughty2.

